The problem statement itself contains a complete solution sketch embedded in the parenthetical text: "Let the centers be A, B, C and radii a, b, c. Then if P is a point on the circles of similitude of the circles a, b and b, c, then PA : PB = a : b and PB : PC = b : c. PA : PC = a : c. Hence P is on the other circle of similitude."

This reveals the entire proof strategy: a point P lying on two of the circles of similitude satisfies PA:PB = a:b and PB:PC = b:c, which by transitivity gives PA:PC = a:c, placing P on the third circle of similitude. Since the three circles share common points, they are coaxal. The solution sketch is embedded directly in the problem text, giving away both the answer and the proof method.
